<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kidung Sunda</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Kidung <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunda-strait">Sunda</a> is a Middle Javanese poem preserved in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bali">Bali</a>, probably composed in the sixteenth century. It tells the story of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-bubat">Battle of Bubat</a> of 1357, in which King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/linggabuana">Linggabuana</a> of Sunda and his daughter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dyah-pitaloka">Dyah Pitaloka</a>, who was to marry <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hayam-wuruk">Hayam Wuruk</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/majapahit-empire">Majapahit</a>, were killed with their followers after the Majapahit minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gajah-mada">Gajah Mada</a> demanded her as tribute.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kidung-sunda.jpg" alt="Kidung Sunda" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bali-lontar-kidung-sunda-400ppi.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The poem is in three cantos. In the first, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hayam-wuruk">Hayam Wuruk</a> sends emissaries across the archipelago to find a bride, and a painting of the princess of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunda-strait">Sunda</a> charms him; the poem never names her, though she corresponds to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dyah-pitaloka">Dyah Pitaloka Citraresmi</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pararaton">Pararaton</a>. The official Madhu wins her father's consent, and the Sundanese sail to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/majapahit-empire">Majapahit</a> with some 2,000 vessels, seeing a bad omen as they board. At the port of Bubat the grand vizier <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gajah-mada">Gajah Mada</a> persuades the king not to receive them as equals, holding that Sunda is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a>.</p><p>In the second canto the Sundanese refuse to submit and are slaughtered; the king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunda-strait">Sunda</a> is killed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hayam-wuruk">Hayam Wuruk</a>'s uncles, the kings of Kahuripan and Daha, and the only surviving officer, Pitar, brings the news to the queen and the princess, who kill themselves. In the third, Hayam Wuruk finds the princess dead, laments her and soon dies in misery. His uncles blame <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gajah-mada">Gajah Mada</a> and march on his house, but he puts on religious dress, meditates and vanishes into nothingness.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>All the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> come from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bali">Bali</a>, but it is not known whether the poem was composed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a> or in Bali, nor who wrote it. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> philologist C. C. Berg found several versions and published two, the Kidung Sunda and the shorter Kidung Suṇḍâyana, The Journey of the Sundanese; his edition appeared in 1927. Berg argued for composition around 1550 or later, because the horse of the Sundanese vizier Anèpakěn is compared to Anda Wesi, the horse of Rangga Lawe in the Kidung Rangga Lawe. Mentions of firearms do not settle the date, and Perso-Arabic loanwords such as kabar, news, and subandar, harbourmaster, show Islamic influence.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Scholars treat the poem as a literary work rather than a reliable chronicle, though its events may rest on fact. It is told directly, with lively description and dialogue, and avoids the marvels common in the genre; the one exception, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gajah-mada">Gajah Mada</a>'s disappearance, Nugroho reads as a symbol of death. The narrator takes the Sundanese side, which is why much of the story differs from other sources.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kidung Sunda” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1357, according to the Javanese Pararaton and the later poem <b>Kidung Sunda</b>, King Linggabuana travelled to Majapahit with his daughter Dyah Pitaloka, who was to marry King Hayam Wuruk.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sunda-kingdom">Sunda Kingdom · Military</a></li></ul></section>
